ge source, so the rainbow appears as a grand, full semicircle (or even a circle from an airplane). An artificial light is small and nearby, so any rainbow it creates would be tiny and closer to the light source, often requiring you to be very close to the water spray and the light simultaneously. In summary, artificial lights *can* create rainbow-like effects (e.g., around a bright lamp in fog), but they are rarely as vivid, colorful, or large as a solar rainbow due to lower brightness, limited spectrum, and unfavorable geometry.
